    "summary": "Composite makau (fish-hook) made of bone, and attached to an aho (line) made of thick twisted fibre, coiled into a bundle. \r\nThe hook is made of two pieces of bone respectively forming a shank and a point, each indented and having a knob in the lower section. Both parts are tied together with thin fibre (possibly olonā), which is coiled over a wooden wedge used on one side of the hook as a tightener.\r\nThe shank is straight, with a knob in the upper section, around which coarse fibre is coiled to form a ka&#039;a (snood) connecting to hook to the line. \r\nThe point is slightly curved in, and has an inner barb.\r\nThe number &quot;51&quot; is handwritten onto the shank, in black ink.",
